Falsifies `address_space_identifier::verification::free_releases_exactly_its_own_asid`.

The defect: the mask loses its negation, `&= 1 << bit` in place of `&= !(1 << bit)`. A dropped
`!` is invisible at a glance and the line still reads as "touch this one bit". Freeing an ASID
then keeps that bit and clears every other ASID in the same word of sixty-four.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!(a.bitmap[word] & !(1 << bit), before[word] & !(1 <<
bit))`, which is the "only its own bit" half of the claim. Sixty-three live address spaces would
silently become allocatable again while still running, which is the aliasing the other proof in
this module rules out and this one is what stops arriving by the back door.

diff --git a/crates/address_space_identifier/src/lib.rs b/crates/address_space_identifier/src/lib.rs
index 00d1ed21d..bdbc9a885 100644
--- a/crates/address_space_identifier/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/address_space_identifier/src/lib.rs
@@ -114,3 +114,3 @@ impl Allocator {
         if asid != 0 && (asid as usize) < ASIDS {
-            self.bitmap[asid as usize / 64] &= !(1 << (asid as usize % 64));
+            self.bitmap[asid as usize / 64] &= 1 << (asid as usize % 64);
         }
